Z||000006||tgowens||10-15-2001||08:42 AM||||there are only two wires that go into the sensor....... one is for the coolant light and the other is for the guage. Usually the plug breaks after it becomes brittle because of the heat from the manifold. Check the wiring, if the plug is broken there should be two small silver connectors, one for each pin on the sensor...... it could be that they are reversed.<P>Also, it IS possible to get a bad sensor - it happened to me a couple of weeks ago - installed a brand new one and it was bad...||||reg||1
Z||000007||btoth||10-15-2001||09:51 AM||||If you still can't get it fixed... try taking off the cover for the gauge cluster and make sure the two wiring harnesses that plug in near the bottom on the left and right sides are in good.  If you have a lot of oxidation on the copper, I used the pencil eraser technique on the copper and now mine's all bright and shiny and my lights started working again.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrtgman||10-15-2001||10:04 AM||||It's really odd. The gauge will come down and almost touch 100 once the car has been running for awhile but thats it. Once I turn the car off it pops all the way up (left) again. It's almost like it is not calibrated or something. I guess I'll go back to pep boys and try another one. The wires to the sensor are connected properly. Hmmmmmmm :-)<P>------------------<BR> 86 gt owned since new!<A HREF="http://www.palmbeachloan.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.palmbeachloan.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Zoom88||10-15-2001||10:03 PM|||| Don't know if you have found a fix for your problem. But here is a quick way to check the temperature Sensor that is located on the cylinder head (looking at the motor from the rear of the car it will be on the left hand side of the head just above the exhaust manifold.) Ground both wires from the temperature sensor and then turn the key on, but do not start motor , the gage should peg  to the right and the idiot light should light. If this happens then replace the Temperature sending unit. Their are three temperature sensor/senders, make sure you have the correct one. The one they normally try to give you is the one for the ECM. Borg Warner part number WT3000 THIS IS NOT THE ONE YOU WANT . You can get the one you need at NAPA Part # TS6667 also make sure you have<BR>the wires connected correctly.<P>Zoom Zoom catch me if you can .......<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrtgman||10-15-2001||11:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Zoom88:<BR><B> Don't know if you have found a fix for your problem. But here is a quick way to check the temperature Sensor that is located on the cylinder head (looking at the motor from the rear of the car it will be on the left hand side of the head just above the exhaust manifold.) Ground both wires from the temperature sensor and then turn the key on, but do not start motor , the gage should peg  to the right and the idiot light should light. If this happens then replace the Temperature sending unit. Their are three temperature sensor/senders, make sure you have the correct one. The one they normally try to give you is the one for the ECM. Borg Warner part number WT3000 THIS IS NOT THE ONE YOU WANT . You can get the one you need at NAPA Part # TS6667 also make sure you have<BR>the wires connected correctly.<P>Zoom Zoom catch me if you can .......</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>how do I ground it?<P>------------------<BR> 86 gt owned since new!<A HREF="http://www.palmbeachloan.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.palmbeachloan.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||birdpoo||10-16-2001||04:22 AM||||take a small paperclip and insert it into either female recepticle pins of the plug.<BR>attach a wire to the paperclip and touch it to chassis ground or the engine block.<BR>a jumper wire with aligator clips on both ends works well but any old spare 22-16 gauge should do.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Zoom88||10-16-2001||02:18 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrtgman:<BR><B> <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>how do I ground it?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><FONT COLOR="blue">OK take a small paperclip straighten it out and then bend it into a "U" shape. There should be a plug in type connector that plugs into the temperature sending/sensor unless the connector has long since broken then there is just 2 wires , either way unplug it from the sensor and stick one end of the paperclip into one side of the connector (or one of the wires) and stick the other side of the paperclip into the other side of the connector(or other wire)now with the key turned to the on position ground the paperclip by touching it to something metal on the engine such as the engine lifting ring that is located right above the sensor on the side of the head. As you do this look through the back glass and you may be able to watch and see if the idiot light lights and the gauge should peg, if not get someone to watch for you. If the light lights and the gauge pegs to the right then the gauge, light and wiring are ok, just replace the sender/sensor with the one I told you about earlier., Let me know what you find</FONT>.<P><FONT COLOR="#ff0000">Zoom Zoom ...shifting into 5th and I'm outa here......see ya !!!!</FONT>.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Zoom88 (edited 10-16-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000013||mrtgman||10-16-2001||08:55 PM||||I noticed today at startup that the gauge pegs  to the right while I am cranking the car over.As soon as it starts *bam* back to zero!(left) Should I still do the ground thing or is this a good indication that everything besides the sensor itself is ok?<P>------------------<BR> 86 gt owned since new!<A HREF="http://www.palmbeachloan.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.palmbeachloan.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Phil||10-16-2001||09:03 PM||||Do the ground thing- it's free<P>------------------<BR> <BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_1.gif">     <BR>SC 3800||||reg||1
Z||000015||Zoom88||10-16-2001||09:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phil:<BR><B>Do the ground thing- it's free<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><FONT COLOR="blue">I agree with Phil !!! Yes do the ground thing then you will know for sure !!! This will help with any furthur troubleshooting.</FONT>||||reg||1
